        "problem": "$\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "Let $u={x}^{2}-1.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,\u27f9\\,x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\text{d}u}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n${x}^{2}=u+1.$\n\nNow we can solve the integral in terms of $u,$ and then express our final answer in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (u+1)\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int (u+1){u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{3/2}+{u}^{1/2})\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}+\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-1)^{5}}+\\frac{1}{3}\\sqrt{({x}^{2}-1)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
